WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
The Library of Alexandria didn't fall in one fire, and that surprises most people.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.000
It declined across roughly four centuries, through several separate incidents, and through something much quieter than fire.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.500
Funding. A library that scale needs a state willing to pay scribes, buy scrolls, and keep the roof sound.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:22.000
When Roman civil wars reached Alexandria, the immediate damage was real but it wasn't the whole story.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.500
The deeper damage was decades of instability that made copying and acquiring texts a lower priority.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:33.000
A library isn't a building full of scrolls, it's a building full of scrolls plus a staff maintaining them.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.500
Papyrus degrades. Every scroll needed to be recopied every generation or two or it simply crumbled.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:44.000
Stop paying for that labor and the collection doesn't survive the fire, it dissolves quietly without one.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.500
By the time later disputes and closures happened, much of the original collection was already gone.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.000
The popular version of the story wants a single villain and a single afternoon.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.400
The actual version is a slow withdrawal of institutional attention, which is a much less cinematic kind of loss.
